G703.b:
⟪ἀρετή⟫, -ῆς, ἡ,
[in LXX, in sing.: ⟦Habakkuk 3:3|Hb 3:3⟧, ⟦Zechariah 6:13|Za 6:13⟧ (⁨הוֹד⁩), in pl.: ⟦Isaiah 42:8|Is 42:8⟧, ⟦Isaiah 42:12|12⟧ ⟦Isaiah 43:21|43:21⟧ ⟦Isaiah 63:7|63:7⟧ (⁨תְּהִלָּה⁩), ⟦Esther 14:10|Es 14:10⟧, Wi 4:1 5:13 8:7, II-IV Mac22*;] prop., whatever procures pre-eminent estimation for a person or thing, in Hom. any kind of conspicuous advantage. Later confined by philos. writers to intrinsic eminence—⟪moral goodness⟫, ⟪virtue⟫;
⟪(a)⟫ of God: ⟦2 Peter 1:3|II Pe 1:3⟧;
⟪(b)⟫ of men: ⟦Philippians 4:8|Phl 4:8⟧, ⟦2 Peter 1:5|II Pe 1:5⟧; pl. (⟦Isaiah 42:8|Is 42:8⟧, ⟦Isaiah 42:12|12⟧ ⟦Isaiah 43:21|43:21⟧ ⟦Isaiah 63:7|63:7⟧, ⟦Esther 14:10|Es 14:10⟧), ⟪excellencies⟫: ⟦1 Peter 2:9|I Pe 2:9⟧ (the usage appears to be a survival of an early comprehensive sense in which the original idea is blended with the impression which it makes on others, i.e. praise, renown; v. Hort, I Pe., 129. Deiss., BS, 95 f., thinks it means manifestations of divine power, as in current Gk. speech; cf. also MM, s.v.)†
